# Build context for `services/solver/Dockerfile`, whose context is the REPO ROOT — `contracts/` is a
# sibling of `services/`, and `app.py`'s `parents[4]` anchor requires both trees in their
# repo-relative positions. Almost nothing else in this repository belongs in a Python image.
#
# **Positive list, deliberately.** A deny list would have to name `node_modules` (~934 MB), `.git`,
# `dist`, `.astro`, `.wrangler`, `data/`, `context/`, `docs/`, `e2e/`, `src/`, `public/`,
# `supabase/` AND every secret-bearing path (`.envs/`, `.env*`, `.dev.vars`) — and would silently
# start leaking again the day someone adds a top-level directory. A positive list cannot leak by
# omission; the worst it can do is fail loudly at build time.

# Exclude every top-level entry. `*` matches one path segment, so this reaches no deeper.
*

# The two trees the image needs. Re-including a directory makes its contents eligible again — `*`
# never matched them, being one segment up.
!contracts
!services

# ...minus what must never reach the image, now that `services/` is back in.
#
# `.venv` is the sharp one: 269 MB of ARM64 binaries on this machine, which would silently poison an
# amd64 image (the Dockerfile builds its own venv from `uv.lock`).
services/solver/.venv
# Engine dumps and solve transcripts — development fixtures with no runtime role.
services/solver/data
**/__pycache__
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
